2026-11-12:07:09:04 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   767  We spent some time in class discussing the move from representing languages as explicit sets, to representing languages as the set of strings produced by some generative process, defined by lists of rules. The Chomsky hierarchy defines four language classes of increasing power, corresponding to four constraints on the rule format. 
2026-11-12:07:09:04 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   768  In the box below, A, B, and C are nonterminal symbols, while $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ represent a mix of nonterminal and terminal symbols (with a length of at least one). \textbf{Note}: one entry in each column is a fake.
2026-11-12:07:09:04 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   768  In the box below, A, B, and C are nonterminal symbols, while $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ represent a mix of nonterminal and terminal symbols (with a length of at least one). \textbf{Note}: one entry in each column is a fake.
