2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   807  \solution{(a) $\alpha\rightarrow\beta$ and ``context-friend'' are fake. (b) In order, the rule formats are regular, context-free, context-sensitive, fake, and recursively enumerable. (c) The weakest class is the class of regular languages. One regular language is the set of all integers, which can easily be captured with a rule that generates a digit and another rule that generates both a digit and the nonterminal. The next-weakest class is the class of context-free languages. Note that all regular languages are also context-free, so any regular language is also an example context-free language. The classic example of a language that is context-free but \emph{not} regular is the set of palindromes. (d) The most power language class is the class of recursively enumerable languages. The corresponding rule ($\alpha A \beta \rightarrow \gamma$ means that any sequence of terminals and nonterminals can be rewritten as anything; i.e., this situation is the absence of any constraint on the rules.}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   807  \solution{(a) $\alpha\rightarrow\beta$ and ``context-friend'' are fake. (b) In order, the rule formats are regular, context-free, context-sensitive, fake, and recursively enumerable. (c) The weakest class is the class of regular languages. One regular language is the set of all integers, which can easily be captured with a rule that generates a digit and another rule that generates both a digit and the nonterminal. The next-weakest class is the class of context-free languages. Note that all regular languages are also context-free, so any regular language is also an example context-free language. The classic example of a language that is context-free but \emph{not} regular is the set of palindromes. (d) The most power language class is the class of recursively enumerable languages. The corresponding rule ($\alpha A \beta \rightarrow \gamma$ means that any sequence of terminals and nonterminals can be rewritten as anything; i.e., this situation is the absence of any constraint on the rules.}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   808  \item[3.] What is the first sentence of the Penn Treebank?\hfill{\em (1 point extra credit)}\linebreak
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   809  \solution{\emph{Pierre Vinken, 61 years old, will join the board as a nonexecutive director Nov.\ 29.}. Full credit given if the sentence is more-or-less correct; one point of credit is given if the major gist is there.}{}
