2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   824  \item Consider the above matrix of co-occurrence counts (documents by words). After the application of an appropriate distributional-semantic method (a matrix factorization like SVD, a Bayesian model like LDA, etc), which pair of words, and which pair of documents, will show dramatic increase in similarity?
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   825  \hfill{\em (10 points)}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   826  \solution{$W_1$ and $W_5$, initially with zero similarity, will move closer due to their association with $W_4$, and that shift will in turn draw $D_2$ and $D_4$ closer together.}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   827  {\vspace{5cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   828  \item Would it be more plausible for $W_1$ to be \emph{monosemous} or \emph{polysemous}, and why?\\
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   829  \phantom{x}\hfill{\em (5 points)}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   830  \solution{Polysemous, because while it shifts in sense closer to $W_5$ via $D_2$. its sense in $D_1$ appears distinct in terms of contextual co-occurrence.}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   831  {\vspace{5cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:05 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   832  \end{enumerate}
