2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   923  \end{center}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   924  \begin{enumerate}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   924  \begin{enumerate}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   925  \item What would happen to the Machine Translation (MT) system output if the input context $c_i$ is removed or set to zero? 
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   926  \hfill{\em (4 points)}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   927  \solution{The MT system will not be using any input information from the encoder. It might just hallucinate and not translate.}{\vspace{4cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   927  \solution{The MT system will not be using any input information from the encoder. It might just hallucinate and not translate.}{\vspace{4cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   928  \item Explain what is the intuition behind $\alpha_{ij}$ in the equation $c_i = \sum_j \alpha_{ij} h_j$? 
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   929  \hfill{\em (4 points)}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   930  \solution{$\alpha_{ij}$ represents the relevance/importance of input word at position $j$ for the decoding step $i$. Words with high $\alpha_{ij}$ will be emphasized in weighted average $c_i$ and therefore will impact the decoding of the next word.}{\vspace{4cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   930  \solution{$\alpha_{ij}$ represents the relevance/importance of input word at position $j$ for the decoding step $i$. Words with high $\alpha_{ij}$ will be emphasized in weighted average $c_i$ and therefore will impact the decoding of the next word.}{\vspace{4cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   931  \item Suppose we replaced all $h_j$ (contextual embedding) in the $\alpha_{ij}$ and $c_i$ equations with the input word embeddings instead. What do you think will happen? 
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   932  \hfill{\em (2 points)}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   933  \solution{All reasonable answers will be accepted. For example: The MT system might still work since we are still giving it word embeddings, but it might perform worse since the embeddings are static. Alternative example: The MT system will fail because it can no longer contextual embeddings are extremely important. Yet another example: Transformers do not use RNN encoders, so maybe it will be ok given sufficient training.}{\vspace{5cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   933  \solution{All reasonable answers will be accepted. For example: The MT system might still work since we are still giving it word embeddings, but it might perform worse since the embeddings are static. Alternative example: The MT system will fail because it can no longer contextual embeddings are extremely important. Yet another example: Transformers do not use RNN encoders, so maybe it will be ok given sufficient training.}{\vspace{5cm}}
2026-11-12:07:09:07 macbookphi2115.win.ad.jhu.edu /Users/pkoehn/LaTex/jhu-intro-hlt.github.io/exam   934  \end{enumerate}
